:: Not Enough [event mom_quest] {"position":"4400,1075","size":"100,100"}
/* Quest "Not Enough" (mom_notEnough), STAGE1_PROPOSAL.md 5.4. She comes up to my room in a good mood: her rounds at the office
   are going well. I ask her to take care of me, the way she does now whenever I ask, and halfway through I tell her that her
   hand isn't enough anymore. She says no, and means it: there's a line, and that's it. I scoff and send her away, and decide
   on the spot that she'll find out what it's like when I stop needing her. Before I sleep I set the device in the house to
   keep her guilt going all week. The quest's set starts The Silence ($mom.flags.silence, the meals of her attempts).
   Her note later in the week calls back to this night ("I'm not sure I meant it"). */
<<silent>><<SetBackground $locations[1].img>><</silent>>
She knocks the old way, two taps and a third, and comes in before I answer. She's still in her work blouse with the sleeves rolled up, holding a basket of my laundry on one hip, and she's humming.

$mom.callName hasn't really hummed in years, but she's been humming all week, ever since her rounds at the office started going right.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $mom, text: "Clean socks, honey. All of them matched, even the gray ones, which I want you to know was a spiritual journey." },
{ text: "She sets the basket on my bed and starts putting things into my drawers without asking, because she has never once in my life asked." },
{ who: $mom, text: "$darius.name laughed today, honey, out loud, at something I said, and $ethan.name swears nobody in that office has ever heard him do that before." },
{ who: $player, text: "I saw. Half the office turned around." },
{ who: $mom, text: "You saw? You were watching?" },
{ text: "She pretends to be embarrassed about it, and folds the same shirt twice to prove it." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

<<linkreplace "Ask her to take care of me">>
<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $player, text: "Leave the laundry for a second and come here." },
{ who: $mom, text: "Is it bad today?" },
{ who: $player, text: "Pretty bad, yeah." },
{ who: $mom, text: "Okay, sweetie. Come on, then, let's take care of it." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

She closes the drawer and comes over and sits down next to me on the bed, the way she always does now, on my left, close. Her hand finds my belt with no hesitation at all. A month ago she needed the dial all the way up to get her this far. Tonight she's humming under her breath while she does it.

And that's what bothers me. She's made it hers. She has a rule for it and a place for it in her week, somewhere between the laundry and the client calls, and inside that rule she's perfectly comfortable. I think she honestly believes this is where it stops.

<<linkreplace "Stop her">>
I put my hand over hers.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $mom, text: "What is it, honey, did I do something wrong?" },
{ who: $player, text: "You didn't do anything wrong. It's just not enough for me anymore." },
{ text: "She doesn't understand. Her eyebrows pull together, the way they do when someone on the phone gives her a wrong number." },
{ who: $mom, text: "What isn't, sweetie?" },
{ who: $player, text: "Your hand. I want you to use your mouth." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

She takes her hand back, slowly, as if she wants to be very sure she's the one deciding to do it.

<<linkreplace "Wait for her answer">>
<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $mom, text: "No." },
{ text: "Just that. She sits there with her hands in her lap and lets it be the whole sentence for a while." },
{ who: $mom, text: "Honey, no. I know what I've... I know what we've been doing. I know how it looks, and I know it's not normal, and I've made my peace with it, because you needed me and I'm the one who takes care of you. That's my job. It's always been my job." },
{ who: $mom, text: "But there's a line, sweetheart. I'm still your $mom.relation, and there are things a $mom.relation just doesn't do, and that's one of them, and that's where it stops." },
{ who: $player, text: "Says who?" },
{ who: $mom, text: "Says me." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

Her voice shakes on it, but she holds on. She's looking right at me. I can't remember the last time she said no to me and looked at me while she did it.

$company.prototype.name is in my desk drawer, three feet away and fully charged, and for a second I actually think about opening it.

I leave it shut. If I turned the dial now, I'd probably get her mouth tonight, and her "no" would be back by breakfast, along with the tears and the shower that runs for an hour. What I want is for the "no" to go away, and I'm fairly sure no dial can do that for me. She'd have to get rid of it herself.

<<linkreplace "Scoff">>
I laugh, short and through my nose, the way you'd laugh at a kid who has just explained the very serious rules of a game he made up.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $player, text: "Right. You've decided." },
{ who: $mom, text: "Honey..." },
{ who: $player, text: "You flirt your way around my office every day with three guys you barely know, you took your clothes off in this room, you come up here whenever I need you, all of it because I asked. And this is where you suddenly remember you're my $mom.relation?" },
{ text: "She flinches at the part about the three guys, and again at the part about coming up here." },
{ who: $mom, text: "That's not fair, honey, that's not... I do all of that for you, I've done everything you ever..." },
{ who: $player, text: "Then go to bed." },
{ who: $mom, text: "What?" },
{ who: $player, text: "You said no, and that's fine. Go to bed, $mom.callName. I have work to do." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

I pick my laptop up off the nightstand and open it, and look at the screen.

For a long moment she doesn't move. Then the bed shifts as she stands up. She doesn't go right away. She stands next to the bed, and I can feel her waiting for me to look up, for one word, for anything at all that would let her stay and still have said no.

I scroll through an email I've already read.

<<linkreplace "Let her go">>
<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $mom, text: "I'll... I'll finish the laundry tomorrow." },
{ text: "I don't answer. She picks up the empty basket, and then sets it down again, and then picks it up." },
{ who: $mom, text: "Don't stay up too late with that thing, okay? Goodnight, sweetheart." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

The door closes very quietly behind her. She always closes doors quietly when she's upset, as if the door had done something and might do it again.

I wait until I hear her bathroom door, then the water running. Then I open the desk drawer.

<<DevicePulse 1>>

Somewhere between the laptop and the drawer I've made up my mind: for the next week, I'm not going to need her for anything, not even the laundry.

I don't point $company.prototype.name at her, though. She's started to notice things lately, and I'm afraid she'd feel it. I set it to the whole house instead, low, all night and every night this week, and tune it to something that's already in her and only needs a little warmth to grow: the feeling of having let somebody down. If it works, she'll lie awake with it, and she'll still have it when she comes downstairs to make breakfast.

I close the drawer and go to sleep easily, which surprises me a little.

<<UpdateStat "+" "MomResistance" 5>>
<<UpdateStat "-" "MomRelation" 3>>
<<UpdateStat "+" "Corruption" 2>>
<<Feel $mom "player" -2>>
<<ProfileLog $mom "Not Enough" "I told her that her hand isn't enough anymore. She said no, and looked at me while she said it: she's still my $mom.relation, and that's where it stops. I laughed at her and sent her to bed. For the next week I'm not going to need her for anything.">>
<<NextQuest $mom>>
<<ReturnFromEvent "Continue" "Bedroom">>
<</linkreplace>>
<</linkreplace>>
<</linkreplace>>
<</linkreplace>>
<</linkreplace>>
